{
  "id": "COMPOUND:15830 - PROTEIN:6323",
  "verbalization": "Demethylregelin is shown to have a target relationship with DNA polymerase iota through three distinct pathways, all of which converge on the enzyme as a final target. The first pathway links Demethylregelin to hepatocytes, which are then targeted by a specific compound, (1S,5R,7R,8S)-7-hydroxy-1,5-dimethyl-8-propan-2-yl-9,10-dioxatricyclo[6.2.2.02,6]dodec-2(6)-en-3-one, which in turn targets DNA polymerase iota. The second pathway connects Demethylregelin to CX-1, which is targeted by Neocurcumenol, a compound that also targets DNA polymerase iota. The third pathway involves Demethylregelin interacting with VMRC-LCP, which is targeted by 6-C-Methylquercetin 3-methyl ether, a compound that also targets DNA polymerase iota. These pathways collectively suggest that Demethylregelin may exert its effects through a series of molecular intermediates that ultimately lead to the inhibition of DNA polymerase iota. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Demethylregelin and DNA polymerase iota, though the exact mode of action and the role of the intermediates remain to be fully elucidated.",
